Lab 5: Introduction to Pandas
Objectives
Understand the basic operations in Pandas
Learn how to load data into a DataFrame
Analyze data using built-in functions
Perform data cleaning techniques
Prerequisites
Basic understanding of Python programming
An Ubuntu 24.04 lab VM with sudo and internet access
Python 3.12 and Pandas (you will install them in a virtual environment in this lab; no Jupyter or IDE is needed, only the terminal)
Lab Tasks
Important: Do not blindly copy-paste commands or run scripts. Read each command, understand what it does, then apply it yourself and complete the lab properly in the lab terminal.

Task 1: Load a CSV file into a DataFrame
Ubuntu 24.04 protects the system Python (PEP 668), so pip install outside a virtual environment fails. Install Python tooling and create a venv first. The packages are pinned so everyone gets the same behaviour; matplotlib and scikit-learn are not used in this lab but are installed because later labs in the series rely on them.

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y python3 python3-venv python3-pip
python3 -m venv ~/mlenv
source ~/mlenv/bin/activate
pip install numpy==2.1.3 pandas==2.2.3 matplotlib==3.9.2 scikit-learn==1.5.2
Expected output (last line):

Successfully installed ... numpy-2.1.3 ... pandas-2.2.3 ... scikit-learn-1.5.2
Troubleshooting challenge: If this command fails (for example a package or version cannot be found or built), resolving it is part of your task. Read the error message, then check python3 --version, apt-cache policy python3-venv, which pip (it should point to ~/mlenv), pip debug, and the official page https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/install.html, and search the exact error text before continuing.

Verify the installation (the venv must be active, so your prompt starts with (mlenv)):

python3 -c "import pandas; print(pandas.__version__)"
Expected output:

2.2.3
